<p>Listed in the National Register of Historic Places in 1980, this is one of Mandan's oldest houses. {% person "robert-w-welsh-sr" %}Robert W. Welsh Sr.{% endperson %}, whose family came to Dakota Territory in 1874, built it in 1918. Robert and his wife had two children, Earl and Mary, while living there. Robert's father had served as a soldier at Fort Buford and later at Fort Abraham Lincoln.</p>

<p>Mr. and Mrs. Welsh established Mandan's first door-to-door milk-delivery service. Robert became a Northern Pacific Railroad brakeman in 1896 and was promoted to conductor in 1906. He remained with the railroad until 1938. His associations included the Order of Railroad Conductors, Brotherhood of Railroad Trainmen, Benefit Association of Railway Employees, and Knights of Pythias.</p>

<p>The Evangelical Lutheran Joint Synod of Wisconsin purchased the house in 1943. It served as a parsonage until Larry and Mrs. Ness purchased it in 1979.</p>

<figure>
  <img src="/assets/images/welch-ness-home-interior-1979.jpg" alt="Living and dining rooms of the Welch-Ness Home in October 1979">
  <figcaption>Living and dining rooms in October 1979</figcaption>
</figure>

<p>The Ness family sold the house to Steven and Mrs. Wolff, who purchased it in 1980.</p>

<p>The house is an outstanding regional example of working-class residential architecture from its period. Its subdued exterior and richly finished wooden interior reflect the Welsh family's place in local society. It retains a high degree of integrity in both its exterior and interior details and expresses the exuberance of the post-Victorian era.</p>
